  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Cool northeasterly surface and low level flow has \nbrought widespread stratus into the region this afternoon. We're \ngradually seeing this cloud layer mix-out, which should still result \nin a brief window of highs in the lower 80s.  Further west, lots of \nmid-high level AC moving eastward towards the central Dakotas. These \nclouds, tied to weak dPVA at the 600:500 mb layer, will continue to \npush eastward this evening.  Below this cloud layer, soundings \nindicate a very stout and deep dry layer which should prevent \nanything more than a few sprinkles reaching the ground later this \nafternoon/evening. \n\nTONIGHT:  A very tricky and uncertain forecast continues into \nMonday, as no clear forcing mechanisms to develop convection are \npresent at this point. Despite the pesky northwest flow pattern, \nmost CAMs and global models have backed off on surface based \nconvection over the western Dakotas this afternoon and evening. The \nthick layer of smoke aloft could potentially cause some limitation \non convective development, but also the lack of any clear defined \nshortwave in the flow.  Now with that said, there are a few hints at \nsome weak 700 mb warm advection trying to develop after midnight \n(perhaps closer to 3-4am) into central South Dakota.  A parcel \nlifted off the 700 mb layer should have upwards of 2000 J/KG MUCAPE \nwith upwards of 25 knots of shear.  Thus if a storm develops, it \ncould briefly produce golf ball size hail and some modest wind \ngusts. The eastward movement of this activity beyond the James \nRiver should be limited by lingering low-lvl dry air. Elsewhere,\ngiven low-lvl subsidence continuing into the overnight hours, \nalong with boundary layer dew points in the 70s, there will also\nbe concern for fog/stratus development. \n\nMONDAY: Any morning convection should again weaken by mid-morning, \nleaving potential stratus and fog in place through the morning in a \nlight east southeast wind. Eventually temperatures do rise into the \nlow to middle 80s, but smoke aloft could again limit the rise of \ntemperatures.  There still appears to be a better signal for a \npassing shortwave Monday night into Tuesday capable of producing \nevening convection over the western Dakotas/Nebraska.   The residual \nmoderate elevated instability, and steepening mid-lvl lapse rates, \ncombining with an increasing nocturnal LLJ should be able to produce \none or more clusters of stronger storms moving eastward towards \ncentral SD into the overnight hours. However soundings present more \nand more inhibition as storms move into the CWA. \n\nTUESDAY: A bit of a rinse and repeat forecast for Tuesday as we may \nagain start the day with stratus and some fog and even a few \nlingering elevated thunderstorms. Gradual upslope flow on the \nPlains Tuesday afternoon/evening may again develop one or more \nclusters of convection that may move towards the CWA into \nWednesday morning. \n\nWEDNESDAY: Wednesday continues to look like a quiet day in the \nregion with more influence from minor ridging in the Plains. \nTemperatures with a light wind will rise towards the lower to \nmiddle 80s. By the overnight hours, some potential for MCS \nactivity in KS/NE will attempt to move eastward and could bring \nsome thunderstorm risks to the MO river valley. \n\nTHURSDAY-SATURDAY: Confidence remains rather low in the extended as \nthe mid-lvl pattern shifts from a quasi-northwest flow to zonal by \nthe end of the week. Several waves are likely to traverse these \nwesterlies in the weekend, but a wide array of solutions continues \nto keep lower PoPs (20-30%) for multiple days. In reality, chances \nare likely going to be more limited than the NBM would suggest, \nhowever model spread will need to tighten before a better timing \ncould be had.  Confidence is a bit higher on the slight cooldown of \ntemperatures into the 70s into next week.",
